(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-vascular-pathophysiology-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Vascular Pathophysiology Quiz 2 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which four functions does the vascular system serve?","choices":["Gas exchange and the production of blood cells","Immune surveillance and antibody synthesis","Thermoregulation and acid production","Transport of nutrients, wastes, hormones; homeostasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nutrients go to tissues, wastes come away from them, hormones travel from one part of the body to another, and fluid balance is maintained across all tissues so cells can function. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is an aneurysm?","choices":["Blood tunnelling through the medial planes of the wall","An intimal lesion projecting into the lumen","Localised abnormal dilation of a blood vessel","An irregular direct connection between artery and vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It may be congenital or acquired, and it is classified by shape. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which risk factor for deep vein thrombosis reflects reduced blood flow?","choices":["Immobility","Dehydration","Oral estrogen","Surgery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside increased venous pressure, mechanical injury, increased blood viscosity, anatomic variations and increased coagulability. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the structure of an atheroma?","choices":["A calcified nodule within the media","A soft lipid core with a fibrous cap","A blood-filled channel within the wall","A thin-walled outpouching of the vessel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and these intimal lesions project out into the lumen of the vessel, which is how the plaque converts a wall abnormality into a flow abnormality. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What appearance do affected vessels have on angiography?","choices":["A smooth tapering narrowing","A blood-filled false channel alongside the true lumen","A string of beads","A saccular outpouching from one wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, produced by alternating focal thickening and intervening normal or dilated segments. The renal arteries are the classic example. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which inflammatory marker appears among the risk factors?","choices":["Erythrocyte sedimentation rate","C-reactive protein","Procalcitonin","Ferritin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, listed under inflammation alongside hyperlipidaemia, lipoprotein a, smoking, hypertension, diabetes mellitus and metabolic syndrome. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What underlies aneurysm and dissection at the level of the wall?","choices":["Compromised wall connective tissue","Excess elastin deposition stiffening the wall","Lipid accumulation beneath the endothelium","Bacterial colonisation of the intima"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Arterial walls are constantly remodelling, so any compromise in that connective tissue can produce either lesion, with histopathological change following. Either its structure or its function. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What does the pulmonary loop do?","choices":["Carries deoxygenated blood from the right heart to the lungs","Carries oxygenated blood from the left heart to the tissues","Returns deoxygenated blood from the tissues to the right heart","Supplies the heart muscle itself with blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The systemic loop is the mirror image: highly oxygenated blood from the left heart to the tissues, removing wastes and returning deoxygenated blood to the right heart. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which subtypes of small-vessel arteriosclerosis are related to hypertension?","choices":["Fibromuscular and intimal","Medial and adventitial","Saccular and fusiform","Hyaline and hyperplastic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the vessels involved are the small arteries and arterioles, where downstream ischaemic injury is the consequence. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What triggers renin secretion?","choices":["A rise in pressure in the afferent arterioles","A fall in pressure in the afferent arterioles","A rise in plasma sodium concentration","Direct sympathetic stimulation of the adrenal medulla"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The kidney senses the pressure drop at the afferent arteriole and responds, which is the first step of the sequence that restores it. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does deep vein thrombosis lead to pulmonary embolus?","choices":["The thrombus propagates continuously up the venous system","Fibrosis of the vein raises pulmonary pressure directly","The thrombus dislodges and travels to the pulmonary artery","The clot crosses into the arterial circulation at the capillaries"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, moving freely through the venous system until it reaches the pulmonary artery and occludes it &mdash; an arterial occlusion produced by a venous disease. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is Monckeberg medial sclerosis, and what is its significance?","choices":["Calcification of the media of muscular arteries","Lipid plaque formation with a necrotic core","Hyaline thickening of arterioles from hypertension","Inflammatory destruction of the vessel wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is one of the four patterns of arteriosclerosis, and the notable thing about it is precisely that it usually does not matter clinically. It is usually not clinically significant. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What characterises the arteries?","choices":["Low pressure, thin walls, and a role as a blood reservoir","High pressure, strong walls, high flow velocity","Control conduits releasing blood into the capillaries","Collection of blood from capillaries into larger vessels"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the contrast with veins is instructive: veins are low pressure with thin walls and serve as the major reservoir of extra blood. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which sex and age groups are listed as at risk?","choices":["Premenopausal women specifically","Children and adolescents","Men and postmenopausal women","Men only, at any age"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, together with increasing age &mdash; the pairing implies that the premenopausal state is relatively protective. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Where does deep vein thrombosis rank among cardiovascular causes of death?","choices":["First most common","Third most common","Tenth most common","It is not a significant cause of death"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which places a venous disease immediately behind the arterial ones in mortality &mdash; the venous side is not the minor partner it might appear. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What defines a dissection?","choices":["Localised abnormal dilation of the vessel","Inflammation of the vessel wall with leukocyte infiltration","Calcification of the media without luminal change","Blood enters a wall defect and tunnels through medial planes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct &mdash; through medial or medial-adventitial planes. The vessel is not merely dilated; its wall has been split into layers by blood under pressure. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which conditions predispose to aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Varicose veins and hemorrhoids","Raynaud phenomenon and cold exposure","Atherosclerosis, hypertension and smoking","Thrombocytosis and dehydration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and all three converge on the same endpoint by different routes &mdash; plaque, mechanical stress and an accelerant of both. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What regulates vascular resistance?","choices":["The arterioles, by neural and hormonal input","The capillaries, by altering their permeability","The veins, by varying their capacitance","The adventitia, by contracting around the vessel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is described as a delicate balance between vasoconstrictors and vasodilators rather than a single controlling signal. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What do vascular smooth muscle cells synthesise?","choices":["Collagen, elastin, proteoglycans and cytokines","Immunoglobulins","Clotting factors for the plasma","Surfactant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why they are central to both repair and disease: the same synthetic capacity that rebuilds a wall also builds an atherosclerotic plaque. Growth factors too. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the role of the arterioles?","choices":["Exchange of fluid, nutrients and wastes with the tissues","Transport of blood back toward the heart","Collection of blood emerging from the capillary bed","Control conduits for release of blood to the capillaries"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is the same property that makes them the regulators of vascular resistance, which is one of the two determinants of blood pressure. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What follows injury to the vessel at the start of the atherosclerotic sequence?","choices":["Calcification of the extracellular matrix","Accumulation of low-density lipoprotein in the vessel wall","Smooth muscle proliferation with matrix production","Formation of a fibrous cap over a lipid core"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and monocyte adhesion to the endothelium and platelet adhesion follow it before any smooth muscle is recruited. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How can narrowing of a vessel lumen arise?","choices":["Only progressively over years","Progressively or precipitously","Only precipitously, as a sudden event","Only as a consequence of external compression"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the distinction matters because a gradual stenosis allows collateral supply to develop while a sudden one does not. Atherosclerosis narrows progressively; thrombosis or embolism precipitously. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which layer are smooth muscle cells the predominant element of?","choices":["The intima","The adventitia","The basement membrane","The media"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and their dual role is the point: they are important in normal vascular repair AND play a large part in the development of atherosclerosis. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How does the small vessel group subdivide?","choices":["ANCA-associated, and immune complex","Hyaline and hyperplastic","Saccular and fusiform","Superficial and deep"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with systemic lupus erythematosus and rheumatoid arthritis given as the immune complex examples. The first is a necrotising vasculitis. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What does the adventitia contain?","choices":["A single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane","Lamellar units of elastin and smooth muscle","Nothing &mdash; it is a potential space","Loose connective tissue, sometimes with nerve fibres"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and in large vessels it carries its own small arterioles, which perfuse the adventitia and part of the media &mdash; the wall is too thick to be fed from the lumen alone. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which three are named as weakening the wall through smooth muscle loss or inappropriate matrix synthesis?","choices":["Marfan syndrome, lupus and rheumatoid arthritis","Smoking, diabetes and hyperlipidaemia","Ischaemia, hypertension and tertiary syphilis","Giant cell arteritis, polyarteritis nodosa and Kawasaki disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they form one of three routes to wall failure, alongside poor connective tissue quality and an imbalance of collagen degradation against synthesis. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What combination is essential hypertension suspected to arise from?","choices":["A single inherited defect in the renin gene","Autoimmune destruction of the vascular endothelium","Small changes in renal sodium handling and vessel tone","Chronic infection of the arterial wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why no single lesion is found: the abnormality is distributed across two systems rather than located in one. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What causes varicose veins?","choices":["Thrombus formation within a deep vein","Chronic high intraluminal pressure","Inflammation of the vessel wall by immune complexes","Direct connection between an artery and a vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, producing dilated tortuous veins. Insufficiency of the venous valves is what allows that pressure to build. The wall support is weakened as well. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What produces the colour sequence in Raynaud phenomenon?","choices":["Haemorrhage into the skin followed by resorption","Deposition of immune complexes in dermal vessels","Progressive thrombosis of digital arteries","Vasoconstriction, anoxia, then reperfusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the sequence maps onto the colours in order: white to blue to red. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What happens to the aortic wall in dissection?","choices":["The laminar planes of the media split apart","The intima thickens progressively, narrowing the lumen","The media calcifies without affecting the lumen","The whole circumference dilates uniformly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and hypertension is the major risk factor, though Marfan syndrome and inflammation can also produce it. A blood-filled channel forms within the wall. — SV Vascular Pathophys I Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 29"}]};
